WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.684
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.684 --> 00:00:05.940
we help some ML video experts
with their download speeds.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.860
Will we hit the target bitrate?

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.286
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.286 --> 00:00:11.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.114
Video Miner is a video
processing and ML company.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:16.530
And they came to
me with an issue.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.321
It was taking about
two minutes to transfer

00:00:18.321 --> 00:00:20.250
a video from cloud
storage to a VM,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.110
where computation could occur.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.690
Now 120 seconds may not
sound like a long time

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.430
to transfer a 4-gig video.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.130
But when you're paying for the
CPU while that's downloading,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.374
it feels like an eternity.

00:00:30.374 --> 00:00:32.040
Worse off is that one
of their engineers

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.770
was reporting that curl
was out performing gsutil

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.980
for the downloads, which
didn't sound right to me.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.140
So of course, I wanted to
see the problem myself.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.630
So I set up a small test
that downloaded videos

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.600
of different sizes from a
cloud storage bucket to a VM

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.940
in US Central 1 and
actually confirmed

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.590
their performance findings.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.600
Asking around, it seemed
as though the challenge

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.390
with gsutil is that the
default settings don't line up

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.520
well for large file downloads.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.060
By default, gsutil will spread
downloads across four threads,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.100
but only use one process.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.920
For copying a single
file on a powerful VM,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.990
we can improve that
performance by limiting

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.790
the number of threads,
forcing the system

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.280
to use multiple
processes instead.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.250
To do this, we can specify
a command line option

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.147
to reduce the thread count to 1.

00:01:16.147 --> 00:01:18.480
Now, with this small change,
we improve the performance,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.420
but we're still not at the
level that curl is showing

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.280
when fetching through HTTP.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.850
So for large files, there's a
nifty feature in gsutil that

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.400
should help us out a bit more.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.770
Gsutil uses HTTP
range get requests

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.470
to perform sliced
downloads in parallel when

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.470
downloading large objects
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.390
Along with setting
the thread count to 1,

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.040
we will also set the number
of max components to 8.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.210
This gives us a much
improved per download speed.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.460
So if you're interested in
using either of these flags

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.834
to improve
performance, check out

00:01:48.834 --> 00:01:50.702
the article linked in
the description below.

00:01:50.702 --> 00:01:53.160
And don't forget to check out
the rest of Cloud Performance

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.119
Atlas content.

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:55.660
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.180
every millisecond counts.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.930
